{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, C = 1.","reason":"The digit sums are 19 + A + B and 15 + A + B + C. Their difference C - 4 must be divisible by 3, so C can be 1, 4, or 7. Only 1 is listed, and A = 0, B = 2, C = 1 satisfies both divisibility conditions.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2014_AMC_8_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p153"],"id":"compare-digit-sums","label":"Compare the two digit sums","methodDirectNodeIds":{"compare-digit-sums":["cnm_p155"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p155","cnm_p153"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (4)","reason":"The first and last digit must be 5, giving 5b5b5. Its digit sum is 15 + 2b, divisible by 3 precisely when b is 0, 3, 6, or 9. All four choices satisfy the distinct-digit condition.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_8_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"primary","label":"Apply divisibility by five and three to the alternating digits","methodDirectNodeIds":{"primary":["cnm_p154","cnm_p155"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p154","cnm_p155"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 96","reason":"There are 8 allowed hundreds digits and 4 allowed odd units digits: 1, 5, 7, 9. The allowed tens digits split into residue classes {0,6,9}, {1,4,7}, and {2,5,8} modulo 3. For every hundreds-units pair, exactly 3 tens digits make the digit sum divisible by 3. The count is 8 · 4 · 3 = 96.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12B_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"digit-remainder-count","label":"Choose the outer digits first","methodDirectNodeIds":{"digit-remainder-count":["cnm_h231","cnm_p155","cnm_p156"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h231","cnm_p155","cnm_p156"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, A=9","reason":"Even digits and 5 cannot work. Digits 1 and 7 give digit sums 6 and 12, so those numbers are divisible by 3. Also, 202103=11·18373. Since exactly one digit works, it must be 9.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_Fall_AMC_12A_Q04","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"eliminate-by-divisibility","label":"Eliminate composite possibilities","methodDirectNodeIds":{"eliminate-by-divisibility":["cnm_p157","cnm_p154","cnm_p155","math:modular-congruence"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p157","cnm_p154","cnm_p155","math:modular-congruence"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 1/2","reason":"The four digits sum to 10. Omitting 1 or 4 gives a digit sum divisible by 3, whereas omitting 2 or 3 does not. Each omitted digit is equally likely, so the probability is 2/4 = 1/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_8_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_310","cnm_309","cnm_p153","cnm_p165"],"id":"omitted-digit","label":"Track the digit left in the bag","methodDirectNodeIds":{"omitted-digit":["cnm_285","cnm_p155","cnm_h231"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_285","cnm_p155","cnm_h231","cnm_310","cnm_309","cnm_p153","cnm_p165"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 251,750,000","reason":"N=2,014,000,000=2^7·5^6·19·53. Its five smallest positive divisors are 1, 2, 4, 5, and 8. Thus its fifth-largest divisor is N/8=251,750,000.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2014_AMC_10B_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p155"],"id":"complementary-divisors","lab